# UST.G3FY: The Selected P-delta Socle-Kernel Source Theorem

## Result

Let

\[
R=\mathbb Q[x,y,z]/(F_{6,x},F_{6,y},F_{6,z})
\]

be the Jacobian ring of the selected smooth plane sextic and let
\([P_\Delta]\in R_6\) be the normalized splitting-conic residue class. Then

\[
\dim R_6=19,\qquad \dim R_{12}=1,
\]

and multiplication by the residue has

\[
\operatorname{rank}\bigl(\mu_{P_\Delta}:R_6\to R_{12}\bigr)=1,
\qquad
\dim\ker\mu_{P_\Delta}=18.
\]

This is an exact characteristic-zero statement. It follows from smoothness of
the selected sextic, the complete-intersection Hilbert series

\[
\frac{(1-t^5)^3}{(1-t)^3},
\]

and the previously certified nonzero socle product
\([P_\Delta]^2=(2/375)[\operatorname{Hess}(F_6)]\).

## Why The Count Matters

The kernel dimension is exactly the number of primitive degree-15 generators
in UST.G3FR. This is the first upstream geometric structure found with the
correct unexplained count. It does **not**, by dimension alone, identify the
two spaces.

The emitted good-reduction witness at \(p=21817,\gamma=5\) gives an explicit
rank-18 basis of the kernel and compares it with the selected G3FR lane.

## Direct-Source Exclusion

Three natural constructions fail at the exact anchor chart:

1. Raw products \(A_\theta\ker\mu_{P_\Delta}\), for all three selected
   \(A_\theta\), are rank 18 and disjoint from the selected primitive plane.
2. Divided-power contraction by every selected source
   \(A_\theta M_{12}\) fails, where \(M_{12}\) ranges over all 11 degree-12
   monomials in \(q,G_3,H_4,P_\Delta\).
3. Ordinary differential contraction by the same 33 sources also fails.

The stronger test allows a common linear combination of all 33 sources. Its
target-containment obstruction has rank 33 under each contraction convention,
so its nullspace is zero.

## Interpretation

The 18-count is structural, but the selected compiler is not direct polynomial
multiplication or either standard apolar contraction. The map must use data not
present in that algebraic family, such as a residue projection, a connection
term, or a derived/cohomological transfer. This narrows the construction target
without promoting a numerical coincidence into a theorem.

